<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#muhtasib">Glossary</a> · Term · office</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhtasib</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as amil al-suq, sahib al-suq, muhtesip</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A muhtasib was the holder of the office of hisbah in classical Islamic administrations: a supervisor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bazaar">bazaars</a> and trade and inspector of public places and behaviour in towns, appointed by the sultan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imam">imam</a> or another authority to see that public business followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharia">sharia</a>. The office is commonly held to succeed the Byzantine agoranomos, a market overseer also known in Parthian and Sassanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> the muhtasib regulated weights, money, prices, public morals and cleanliness, and had cheating merchants whipped and paraded; manuals such as al-Shayzari's guided the work. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali of Egypt</a> abolished the office in Cairo and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in 1837.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhtasib.jpg" alt="Muhtasib" width="330" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Claes Rålamb · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ralamb-93.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the chief qadis served deputy judges, preachers, prayer leaders and the teachers of the many madrasas that sultans and amirs built and endowed in Cairo, Damascus and Jerusalem, and until the 15th century a scholar or qadi usually held the post of <b>muhtasib</b>, the inspector of Cairo’s markets.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mamluk-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Mamluk Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
